joining us now is the former  conservative party leader in Duncan  Smith thank you so much for being on the  program  um I'm very keen to talk to you about  China but just first we've just heard  from the German ambassador and I was  quite interested in what he said on  brexit he said that he was quite  optimistic that there would be a change  in tone on the talks on Northern Ireland  that there will be a closer relationship  between the EU and the UK and what's  your sort of sense of where Rishi senek  is when it comes to president well I  think the truth is it's not really where  Rishi sunak is I think because I think  he would love to have a good engagement  I think there's a big question mark for  what the European Union wants to do  because in the protocol it's quite clear  that two things one Good Friday  agreement trumps everything else and at  the moment we have a problem because  consent doesn't take place in Northern  Ireland so which is why the unions have  walked away from the assembly and the  second thing is very important article  13.8 make it absolutely clear if there  are any problems with the protocol the  protocol can be changed in whole or in  part right right now Mr ceskovich does  not have the power to negotiate on 13.8  which is critical because it's not  working so what we're doing at the  moment is still a slight kind of  elaborate dance around this at the heart  of this is article 13.8 I know it sounds  technical but it says the protocol can  be changed in whole or in part it's not  working we should be discussing that and  both sides reach an agreement which  allows them to respect each other's  Market requirements I think that's the  key okay interesting but how slightly  further away than a previous one maybe  not yeah um okay and now you are one of  the most outspoken critics of China  you've previously been sanctioned by  them for speaking out about the uyghurs  so I'm interested to know your thoughts  on the you know the extraordinary scenes  we've seen recently are people taken to  the streets to protest uh initially of  course about the anti-lockdown rules of  the sort of lockdown rules um do you  think change could be happening in China  I I we the trouble is I think with all  of us in the west is that we're  basically all decent people who believe  in democracy Freedom human rights all  the rest of it whether you're in Germany  or in France or in Britain or wherever  on America our tendency is we always  want to believe that the good is coming  and that somehow the natural order of  things is democracy human rights and the  rule of law but with China I think we  have to have a reality check this is a  brutal dictatorial government that  thinks nothing of incarcerating people  it's carrying out genocide amongst the  uyghur in xinjiang I mean literally  slave labor products we buy have got  slave labor in them here all around the  Western World we've done very little to  stop that so this is a a government that  thinks nothing of cracking down so their  control is total they use technology in  a way that we just couldn't imagine over  here all of that tells me that they're  not going anywhere soon they are  determined that their former government  is right and they will not let up so  once they won't modify around the edges  because the covert issues causing them  economic problems as well I don't think  we should overread this at all president  XI is absolutely determined that the  form of government China has is the  formal government for everywhere and  he's selling it to the uh the developing  world right now you're very strong in  your condemnation of what you see it's a  brutal regime what ship of the UK  government response how should we go  about our relationship well I'm deeply  disappointed in my government over this  particular area because of course you  know president the Prime Minister said  during the summer that he thought that  China represented a systemic threat and  then recently he shifted that to  systemic challenge the problem with  challenges it looks weak and we've now  got a policy which it sounds like it  could have come from Sir Humphrey in Yes  Minister which is robust pragmatism now  if somebody can please tell me what  robust pragmatism means it means nothing  robust but also pragmatic they cancel  each other out the reality we've got to  do is we've got to change our integrated  review to put China in as a systemic  threat the Americans are way ahead of us  on this other countries in Europe are  talking about it we the UK could risk  being left behind dragging our feet it  from that everything flows from that  about how we relate to China what does  that mean in Practical terms I mean I  know you've talked a bit about smart  meters for example before are there any  kind of concrete examples of what  acknowledging or saying that China is a  systematic threat would mean being a  systemic threat then going into the  integrated route as a threat as Russia  was means that everything that the  government does then flows from that  point so it means that you can no longer  be ambivalent about what China does you  have to view it in the light of it being  a threat so that means for example we've  got illegal I think they're illegal but  the government seems to turn a blind eye  to them Chinese police stations bullying  Chinese expats to go back they're not  alone in this they're in all sorts of  countries Holland the Netherlands  Germany all these countries but they're  being much more aggressive about it the  Holland has shut down uh theirs the  other day America's kicking them out yet  we seem to be completely ambivalent  about whether or not they're doing this  bullying having video links back to  Beijing with their families brought in  being threatened in front of them if  they don't go back we've got this new  Embassy complex that they want to build  with you the biggest Embassy complex in  the whole of Europe right in the heart  of Europe right next door to the city of  London you know really astonishingly  inept if the government now allows the  appeal to go through we can go on all  these things Confucius Institute spying  on Chinese students our University is  completely dependent on Chinese money  and us as an economy far too dependent  on China a digital regime none of this  can happen unless we see them as a  threat that's the key the pragmatism is  the economic reality of it right that's  how some would see it at a time when you  know we'll look spiral cancer we are in  a recession do we not need to have that  trade relationship with China for the  good of the economy well it depends what  your trade-off is I think this is a  trade-off in human lives  for me the uyghur being  incarcerated the women sterilized  forcibly sterilized often raped we've  got children in re-education camps we've  got uh just a ton of men in forced labor  camps thousands of miles away from their  family never seeing their family again  that's just one bit of it you've got  Tibet forced labor camps you've got the  Christian churches the Evangelical  Church has been cracked down on arrests  taking place Hong Kong you know people  being arrested for their peaceful  democracy campaigns I mean where do you  draw the line do we turn a blind eye to  all of that and say we just go on making  money out of China my answer is  diversify bring some of these  Technologies back to to Europe to the  continent of Europe to Britain to  America actually start looking at India  and others to invest more in to develop  them as a counterbalance can't go on  turning a blind eye to the fact that our  cheaper products come from countries  that use slave labor not on um I just  want to talk to you a little bit as if  at the end about the state of the  conservative party and polling's still  looking pretty difficult to say the  least are you obviously lost the Chester  by-election that itself wasn't a  surprise but labor achieved their  greatest result there for 200 years  um Nadim zahari said that looking at the  pulse he thinks kirst armor should be  worried do you think that's right well  interestingly uh John Curtis the day  after said that whilst this would give  labor this result probably a majority he  didn't say a massive majority he was his  point was making that you'd have  expected them to do better I'm not now  going to be clever about this but the  truth is if you're an opposition you  should be winning a by-election like  that midterm with all our problems right  now by a massive degree and it wasn't as  big now having said that we lost it so  the conservatives shouldn't go around  telling themselves all is okay because  it's going to come right in here it's  like you're  there's about 1200 I think yeah you  could be in trouble yeah well I've been  told I'm in trouble for the last three  elections but I've managed to get back  um all I would say is that conservative  should be complacent we have to get this  economy growing at the heart of this  life people fears about two things I  think one is obviously about migration  the boat's crossing the channel which is  headline stuff and really worrying  people the second bit importantly though  is growth all the economies of Europe  facing very much the same struggle but  the UK has got now the highest tax rate  that it's had for a generation and we've  got to get that down so we need growth  back in the economy easily by next  spring so that we can start to put these  things right and people can feel better  off with access to jobs and to good  incomes these are the key things that  will win us through at the end not  tokenistic excuses about the fact it  could have been better for labor than it  was  um you of course know what it's like to  lead the conservative party in the  depths of opposition do you think you  might be heading back into that  Wilderness all political parties in  government face the prospect of losing  the next election because elections are  about your record often more than they  are about the alternative so the key  question for us is to get the choice  much more difficult for the public  otherwise we would end up going into  opposition and that means making them  make a choice and if we have growth if  we have an economy going forward if you  have people getting jobs livelihoods  looking better people were able to pay  their mortgages and we get that  migration issue under control then we're  in at the races and we have a chance of  winning back into government okay it  sounds like a tough Challenge and thank  you very much for being on the program  and speaking I say strongly about China  as well thank you

